tests/ir/pipeline.sky:7:5: note: [FOLD-FLOAT-001] `2 * 3` is rewritten to `6`
  |
7 | a = 2 * 3
  |     ^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `fold` pass
  = note: because every operand is a constant
  = note: because the result is the reference interpreter's own, bit for bit, and it cannot fail at run time

tests/ir/pipeline.sky:8:10: note: [FOLD-FLOAT-001] `6 * 4` is rewritten to `24`
  |
8 | unused = a * 4
  |          ^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `fold` pass
  = note: because every operand is a constant
  = note: because the result is the reference interpreter's own, bit for bit, and it cannot fail at run time

tests/ir/pipeline.sky:8:10: note: [DCE-001] `a * 4` is removed
  |
8 | unused = a * 4
  |          ^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `dce` pass
  = note: because `unused` is never used
  = note: because `const.float` has no side effects (it cannot print, fail, call or loop), so removing it cannot change what the program does

tests/ir/pipeline.sky:9:5: note: [ALG-001] `x * 1` is rewritten to `x`
  |
9 | y = x * 1
  |     ^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `egraph` pass
  = note: because multiplying a finite float by 1 gives it back bit for bit, and cannot overflow

tests/ir/pipeline.sky:10:5: note: [ALG-007] `-(-t)` is rewritten to `t`
   |
10 | z = -(-t)
   |     ^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `egraph` pass
   = note: because negating a number only flips its sign bit, so applying it twice restores every bit; negating an array is 0 - x per entry, which turns -0 into +0, so for an array the entries must be proven not -0

tests/ir/pipeline.sky:12:7: note: `A⁻¹ @ b` is rewritten to a Cholesky solve
   |
12 | sol = A⁻¹ @ b
   |       ^^^^^^^
   |       - positive definite
   = note: rewrite `A⁻¹ @ b` to a Cholesky solve: A is Matrix[3,3] and PositiveDefinite, b is Vector[3]. Factor A = L·Lᵀ (L lower-triangular), solve L·y = b, then Lᵀ·x = y. x = A⁻¹·b is Vector[3], and A⁻¹ is never formed.
